# Environment contract for ebook-reader (single file, repo root).
# Copy to `.env` and fill every variable not marked optional — both apps
# validate on startup and refuse to run if one is missing or malformed.

# --- Web (Vite) -----------------------------------------------------------
# Must be prefixed VITE_ to be exposed to the client. Loaded from this root
# file via Vite's envDir (see apps/web/vite.config.ts).
# In development this is the web dev server's own /atrium-api, which it proxies
# to the API (D54), so the page and the API share an origin as they do in the
# deploy. The path prefix is kept: apiUrl() in api-client.ts preserves it.
VITE_API_URL=http://localhost:5173/atrium-api

# The sub-path the web app is served under. The deploy sets it itself; locally
# it makes the dev server match the deploy. Open http://localhost:5173/atrium/.
BASE_PATH=/atrium/

# --- API (Fastify) --------------------------------------------------------
PORT=3001
HOST=0.0.0.0

# --- Accounts (D58) -------------------------------------------------------
# Atrium has its own accounts: a username and password per person, stored in
# the API's database by Ward's vendored library (apps/api/vendor/ward). There
# is no Register page. Create accounts with the seed script, which reads the
# values from the environment or prompts for them, never from a file:
#
#   SEED_USERNAME=... SEED_PASSWORD=... SEED_ROLE=member npm run seed:user -w @ebook-reader/api
#
# SEED_ROLE is `member` (full access, the default) or `jukebox` (the Discord
# bot's allowlist, D55).

# Atrium's public origin as the browser sees it, no path. REQUIRED. The
# session cookie is `Secure` unless this is plain HTTP on loopback, and a
# browser POST to the sign-in routes from any other origin is refused.
# Locally the web dev server's origin; in the deploy e.g. https://example.com.
PUBLIC_ORIGIN=http://localhost:5173

# The proxies whose X-Forwarded-For the password sign-in lockout believes:
# addresses or CIDR ranges, comma-separated. SET IT WHEREVER ATRIUM RUNS BEHIND
# A PROXY THAT IS NOT ON LOOPBACK, which is every container deploy. There the
# socket peer is the Docker bridge, not the client, and with this unset every
# client shares one failure counter: six wrong passwords from anyone lock the
# owner, the household and the Discord bot out of password sign-in for 15
# minutes. A client cannot spoof its way round it: the header is read only from
# a trusted peer, and only its last entry (the one the proxy appended).
#
# 172.16.0.0/12 holds Docker's default bridge networks (172.17-172.31). If the
# deploy's network sits elsewhere (`docker network inspect`), name its subnet
# instead. A log line "X-Forwarded-For ignored for the login lockout" means the
# proxy's network is missing here. Loopback is always trusted, so locally (the
# web dev server proxies from 127.0.0.1) the value is harmless. With
# NODE_ENV=production and this unset, the API warns at boot.
AUTH_TRUSTED_PROXIES=172.16.0.0/12

# --- Sign in with Ward (optional) -----------------------------------------
# A second way in: an OpenID Connect flow against Ward, the estate's identity
# service. Leave WARD_OIDC_ISSUER unset and atrium runs on its own accounts
# only, with no Ward button. With the issuer set, every other variable below
# but the last is required, and the API refuses to boot without them.
#
# Locally, Ward's container (wzd_auth/infrastructure/local) writes all five
# into .env with its seed.mjs and registers atrium as a client. In the deploy,
# Ward's console registers the client and the deploy provides the values.

# Ward's issuer, ending in /oidc. Plain http only on loopback.
WARD_OIDC_ISSUER=http://localhost:8792/ward-api/oidc

# Atrium's client id at Ward: its app slug.
WARD_OIDC_CLIENT_ID=atrium

# Atrium's Ed25519 private key, base64url of a JSON JWK. A SECRET: server-side
# only, never a VITE_ variable, never logged. Ward's `npm run oidc-client-key`
# prints a fresh pair (this value, and the public half for Ward's console).
WARD_OIDC_CLIENT_KEY=

# The callback as the browser reaches it, registered at Ward exactly. It lives
# under the API prefix, so the session cookie reaches it and no new proxy rule
# is needed: <PUBLIC_ORIGIN>/atrium-api/auth/ward/callback.
WARD_OIDC_REDIRECT_URI=http://localhost:5173/atrium-api/auth/ward/callback

# Optional: where Ward returns after "Sign out of Ward too". Registered at Ward.
WARD_OIDC_POST_LOGOUT_REDIRECT_URI=http://localhost:5173/atrium/

MAX_UPLOAD_MB=50

# Ceiling on a library conversion job (brief 34 / D34), in milliseconds.
# 24 hours. That is a last-resort reaper, not a UX guard: nobody waits at the
# screen for a conversion — the book's row says "running", the client polls, and
# the person walks away. This number exists only so a wedged `ebook-convert`
# can't pin a CPU forever. The answer to "this is taking too long" is the Cancel
# button, not a shorter ceiling.
CONVERT_JOB_TIMEOUT_MS=86400000

# Optional (brief 22): base URL of the Gutendex instance the catalog proxy
# queries. Defaults to the public community instance when unset; override to
# point at a self-hosted mirror. Not required — leave unset for normal use.
# GUTENDEX_BASE_URL=https://gutendex.com

# Optional LaTeX limits (brief 38), not part of the required contract above.
# Each has a sensible default — set one only to tune it.
#
# LATEX_TIMEOUT_MS is the compile route's outer wall-clock backstop, NOT the
# real guard: the typesetting engine is a pure function with a deterministic
# step budget (D38), and that budget is what stops a runaway macro — at the
# same point on every machine. This timer only covers a wedge outside the
# engine's step loop. Two minutes is generous on purpose; a shorter ceiling
# would not add safety, it would just fail slow-but-valid documents on a slow
# machine, non-deterministically.
# LATEX_TIMEOUT_MS=120000
#
# Cap on the compiled PDF one compile may produce. A tiny document can emit a
# huge output, so the project cap below does not imply this one.
# LATEX_MAX_OUTPUT_MB=25
#
# Cap on a project's whole working tree — sources plus every uploaded figure
# and .bib. MAX_UPLOAD_MB bounds a single file; this bounds their sum.
# LATEX_MAX_PROJECT_MB=50

# Optional storage-root overrides, not part of the required contract above.
# Each defaults to a path under apps/api/ when unset. Redirect all five
# together to point a test/deploy at a scratch storage root, so a scratch
# database and scratch files move as one — redirecting only LIBRARY_DATA_DIR
# still leaves LIBRARY_FILES_DIR/THUMBNAILS_DIR pointed at the real files,
# which is how a verification run destroyed a real book on 2026-08-25.
# LIBRARY_DATA_DIR=/tmp/atrium-scratch/data
# LIBRARY_FILES_DIR=/tmp/atrium-scratch/library
# THUMBNAILS_DIR=/tmp/atrium-scratch/thumbnails
# LaTeX project working trees (drafts) and published version artifacts
# (brief 38). Overridable for the same reason as the three above — a root that
# can't be redirected can't be tested destructively (D39).
# LATEX_PROJECTS_DIR=/tmp/atrium-scratch/latex
# DOCUMENT_VERSIONS_DIR=/tmp/atrium-scratch/versions
